.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "why", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. Why Rails Mini Profiler
  :markdown
    Two tools share the name and the hedgehog.
    [rack-mini-profiler](https://github.com/MiniProfiler/rack-mini-profiler)
    is the veteran: framework-agnostic, capable of light production
    sampling, and shown as a floating badge with a timing table. Rails Mini
    Profiler trades the generality for depth. It is Rails-only, it **stores
    every profiled request** in your database so you can compare a request
    before and after a change, and it puts the detail — a real waterfall,
    per-trace popovers, a [Speedscope](https://github.com/jlfwong/speedscope)
    flamegraph — in a dashboard built for reading rather than a tooltip.

    It is a **developer tool, not monitoring**. For "which endpoints are
    slow for real users this week" you want an APM. For "this page takes
    two seconds on my machine and I don't know where", this is the fastest
    route to the answer.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "install", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. Installation
  :markdown
    We install **our fork** from Git — a plain `gem "rails_mini_profiler"`
    would pull the archived original (see [section 6](#fork)). Put it in
    the main group, not `:development`, if you ever want to profile a
    staging or production request; it is inert until enabled. Add
    `stackprof` alongside it for flamegraphs:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # Gemfile
      gem "rails_mini_profiler", git: "https://github.com/profoundry-us/rails-mini-profiler", branch: "main"

      # Flamegraphs for rails_mini_profiler
      gem "stackprof"

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    The installer writes an initializer, a migration for the three tables
    that hold profiled requests, traces, and flamegraphs, and mounts the
    dashboard. Run it, then migrate:

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4") do |code|
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bundle install
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bin/rails rails_mini_profiler:install
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bin/rails db:migrate

.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # config/routes.rb (added by the installer)
      mount RailsMiniProfiler::Engine => "/rails_mini_profiler"

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    One more step that the installer **cannot** do for you. ViewComponent
    does not instrument renders by default, so the component tracer sees
    nothing until the host app turns instrumentation on. Do it in the
    initializer rather than `application.rb` so it can be gated later (see
    [section 5](#production)):

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # config/initializers/rails_mini_profiler.rb
      Rails.application.config.view_component.instrumentation_enabled = true
      Rails.application.config.view_component.use_deprecated_instrumentation_name = false

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Start the app and load any page. A small hedgehog badge appears in the
    corner; click it — or visit `/rails_mini_profiler` — for the dashboard.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "reading", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. Reading a Profile
  :markdown
    The dashboard lists every profiled request with its total time and a
    breakdown. Open one and you get the **waterfall**: a bar per trace,
    positioned in time and nested by what called what, with the time a
    trace spent *itself* (as opposed to in its children) shown explicitly.
    Each bar is a tracer's output:

- # The tracer table.
.mt-4.overflow-x-auto.rounded-box.border.border-base-300
  = daisy_table(css: "table-zebra") do |table|
    - table.with_head do |head|
      - head.with_column { "Tracer" }
      - head.with_column { "What you see" }
    - tracers.each do |tracer, what|
      - table.with_row do |row|
        - row.with_column do
          %code= tracer
        - row.with_column do
          :markdown
            #{what}

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Three habits get you to the answer quickly:

    - **Sort by self time, not total.** The controller bar is always the
      longest; the question is which leaf under it is.
    - **Collapse the tree and look for grouped repeats.** Fifty identical
      `SELECT … WHERE id = ?` rows grouped into one line with a count is
      an N+1, and the popover names the query so you know which
      `includes` to add.
    - **Open the flamegraph when the waterfall is quiet.** If the request
      is slow but no single trace is, the time is in Ruby — serialisation,
      a helper, a gem — and the flamegraph shows the stack that ate it.

= doc_note(modifier: :note, title: "Flamegraphs and Concurrency", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    StackProf cannot sample concurrent requests, so a page that fires
    several requests at once (Turbo frames, assets) may record a flamegraph
    for only one of them. Re-send just the request you care about, or
    append `?rmp_flamegraph=true` to jump straight to its flamegraph.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "config", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. Configuration
  :markdown
    The generated initializer is well commented; three settings are worth
    changing on day one.

    **Turn off per-trace backtraces.** Every trace captures a backtrace for
    the "source" section of its detail view. On a component-heavy page that
    capture measured around **30% of the profiled request** — the profiler
    was the slowest thing in the profile. Leave it off for honest numbers
    and flip it on only when you need to know *where* a trace originates.

    **Skip the noise.** Health checks, asset requests, and the profiler's
    own dashboard don't need profiling. `skip_paths` takes strings or
    regexes.

    **Sweep old requests.** Profiled requests are never deleted for you.
    On a shared staging database the tables grow forever; a small job on a
    schedule keeps them bounded.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # config/initializers/rails_mini_profiler.rb
      RailsMiniProfiler.configure do |config|
        config.enabled = proc { |env| Rails.env.development? }

        # Backtrace capture measured ~30% of a profiled request; the trace
        # detail view just omits its "source" section with this off.
        config.backtraces_enabled = false

        config.flamegraph_enabled = true
        config.skip_paths = ["/up", %r{\A/assets/}]

        # Default set — drop `view_component` if the app doesn't use it.
        config.tracers = %i[controller instantiation sequel view view_component cache job rmp]
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/jobs/profiled_request_cleanup_job.rb
      class ProfiledRequestCleanupJob < ApplicationJob
        def perform
          RailsMiniProfiler::ProfiledRequest.where("created_at < ?", 2.weeks.ago).destroy_all
        end
      end

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "production", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 5. Profiling Beyond Development
  :markdown
    Some slowness only exists with real data. Rails Mini Profiler supports
    this — nothing is profiled and the dashboard is unreachable until you
    say otherwise — but the stock suggestion (a request header) is awkward
    from a browser, and every profiled request costs the server. Our
    pattern is a **key-approved session**:

    1. Visit any URL once with `?rmp_key=<secret>` (the secret lives in an
       `RMP_API_KEY` environment variable).
    2. A small middleware compares it in constant time, stamps the session
       as approved, and authorises the profiler "user" on every later
       request in that session.
    3. `config.enabled` and the dashboard's route constraint both check the
       session flag, so approved sessions are profiled and can read the
       dashboard; everyone else sees no badge, no traces, and a 404 for
       `/rails_mini_profiler`.

    Rotating the key is a config change. The full middleware is about
    forty lines; the shape is:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # config/initializers/rails_mini_profiler.rb
      class RmpApiKeyGate
        SESSION_FLAG = "rmp_authorized"
        ENV_FLAG = "app.rmp_authorized"

        def initialize(app) = @app = app

        def call(env)
          session = env["rack.session"]
          session[SESSION_FLAG] = true if session && key_valid?(env)

          if session && session[SESSION_FLAG]
            env[ENV_FLAG] = true
            RailsMiniProfiler::User.authorize("rmp-api-key")
          end

          @app.call(env)
        end

        private

        def key_valid?(env)
          key = ENV["RMP_API_KEY"].to_s
          given = Rack::Utils.parse_query(env["QUERY_STRING"].to_s)["rmp_key"].to_s
          !key.empty? && !given.empty? && ActiveSupport::SecurityUtils.secure_compare(given, key)
        end
      end

      Rails.application.config.middleware.insert_after(ActionDispatch::Session::CookieStore, RmpApiKeyGate)
      Rails.application.config.filter_parameters += [:rmp_key]

      RailsMiniProfiler.configure do |config|
        config.enabled = proc { |env| Rails.env.development? || env[RmpApiKeyGate::ENV_FLAG] }
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # config/routes.rb
      mount RailsMiniProfiler::Engine => "/rails_mini_profiler",
            constraints: ->(req) { Rails.env.development? || req.session[RmpApiKeyGate::SESSION_FLAG] == true }

= doc_note(modifier: :warning, title: "Instrumentation Is Not Free", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    ViewComponent instrumentation fires a notification on **every**
    component render whether or not the request is being profiled. Enable
    it outside development only when the deployment can actually profile —
    `if Rails.env.development? || ENV["RMP_API_KEY"].present?` — so a
    production app without a key pays nothing. And treat the key as
    rotatable, not secret: it travels in a query string, which your host's
    router may log even though Rails filters it.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "fork", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 6. Why a Fork
  :markdown
    The original project's last release was **v0.7.3 in October 2022**, and
    it profiles the Rails of that era: controllers, SQL, and templates. A
    LocoMotion app renders through components, leans on the cache, and
    enqueues jobs from requests — none of which appeared in a profile. We
    maintain [profoundry-us/rails-mini-profiler](https://github.com/profoundry-us/rails-mini-profiler)
    to fill those gaps, and it is what the guide above installs.

